{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Mocking Async Dependencies.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Mocking Async Dependencies.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"mocking async dependencies mocking async dependencies","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Mocking Async Dependencies.md","fileName":"Mocking Async Dependencies.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Mocking Async Dependencies","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Parallel Diagnostics and Testing]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Parallel Diagnostics and Testing","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Mocking Async Dependencies.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Mocking Async Dependencies.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"mocking async dependencies mocking async dependencies","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Mocking Async Dependencies.md","fileName":"Mocking Async Dependencies.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Mocking Async Dependencies","heading":"Mocking Async Dependencies","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An asynchronous mock should return a completed, faulted, canceled, or deliberately controlled task that matches the dependency's real contract. Returning null for a task or hiding synchronous work behind a misleading asynchronous signature makes the test exercise behavior that production code should never see.\n\nControlled incomplete tasks can verify ordering and cancellation, while completed values keep ordinary tests concise. The mock should preserve important timing-independent semantics rather than imitate incidental implementation delays.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Parallel Diagnostics and Testing","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd"